1
00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:02,120
The checkout page is slow.

2
00:00:02,120 --> 00:00:03,552
Customers are clicking,

3
00:00:03,552 --> 00:00:04,798
and nothing happens.

4
00:00:04,798 --> 00:00:06,169
The servers look fine.

5
00:00:06,169 --> 00:00:06,729
What now?

6
00:00:07,279 --> 00:00:10,239
Very often the answer is inside the database.

7
00:00:10,239 --> 00:00:12,213
A session that never finished.

8
00:00:12,213 --> 00:00:14,054
A lock that nobody released.

9
00:00:14,054 --> 00:00:15,962
A query that reads every row.

10
00:00:16,512 --> 00:00:19,520
Today you look inside a real post gress server,

11
00:00:19,520 --> 00:00:20,992
find the stuck session,

12
00:00:20,992 --> 00:00:21,888
end it safely,

13
00:00:21,888 --> 00:00:23,552
and speed up a slow query.

14
00:00:24,102 --> 00:00:26,641
Look first. Then act on exactly one thing.

15
00:00:28,191 --> 00:00:31,263
Here is what to expect, from the first minute to the last.

16
00:00:31,813 --> 00:00:36,400
One: the words. Connection, session state, lock, blocking, and slow query.

17
00:00:36,950 --> 00:00:41,643
Two: a live demo. We count the connections, and find who is waiting for whom.

18
00:00:42,193 --> 00:00:46,716
Three: we end the one session that blocks everyone, first gently, then firmly.

19
00:00:47,266 --> 00:00:49,298
Four: we find the slowest query,

20
00:00:49,298 --> 00:00:50,187
read its plan,

21
00:00:50,187 --> 00:00:51,775
and fix it with an index.

22
00:00:51,775 --> 00:00:52,727
Then your turn,

23
00:00:52,727 --> 00:00:55,522
with a lab that judges you with five checks.

24
00:00:56,072 --> 00:00:58,845
This is lesson seven of the databases domain.

25
00:00:59,395 --> 00:01:01,934
Look first. Then act on exactly one thing.

26
00:01:03,984 --> 00:01:06,288
Eight ideas for a database in trouble.

27
00:01:06,838 --> 00:01:09,767
A connection is one client talking to the server.

28
00:01:09,767 --> 00:01:11,082
Each one is a session,

29
00:01:11,082 --> 00:01:12,337
with its own process,

30
00:01:12,337 --> 00:01:13,473
and its own memory.

31
00:01:14,023 --> 00:01:15,662
Every session has a state.

32
00:01:15,662 --> 00:01:17,868
Active means it is running a query.

33
00:01:17,868 --> 00:01:20,326
Idle means it is connected and waiting.

34
00:01:20,326 --> 00:01:25,180
And idle in transaction means it started a transaction and never finished it.

35
00:01:25,730 --> 00:01:28,226
A lock protects a row while it changes.

36
00:01:28,226 --> 00:01:31,298
Other sessions that want the same row must wait.

37
00:01:31,298 --> 00:01:32,258
That is normal,

38
00:01:32,258 --> 00:01:33,346
for milliseconds.

39
00:01:33,896 --> 00:01:36,681
Trouble starts when the holder never lets go.

40
00:01:36,681 --> 00:01:40,210
An idle in transaction session can hold a lock for hours,

41
00:01:40,210 --> 00:01:42,067
and everyone behind it queues.

42
00:01:42,617 --> 00:01:43,959
Two ways to end it.

43
00:01:43,959 --> 00:01:46,996
P G cancel backend stops the running query.

44
00:01:46,996 --> 00:01:50,174
P G terminate backend ends the whole session,

45
00:01:50,174 --> 00:01:51,869
and rolls back its work.

46
00:01:51,869 --> 00:01:53,070
Try cancel first.

47
00:01:53,620 --> 00:01:54,736
Resource pressure.

48
00:01:54,736 --> 00:01:56,037
Too many connections,

49
00:01:56,037 --> 00:01:57,153
too little memory,

50
00:01:57,153 --> 00:01:57,897
a full disk,

51
00:01:57,897 --> 00:01:59,260
a low cache hit ratio.

52
00:01:59,260 --> 00:02:00,686
The server has a limit,

53
00:02:00,686 --> 00:02:02,793
and you can see how close you are.

54
00:02:03,343 --> 00:02:06,690
A slow query is usually a query reading too much.

55
00:02:06,690 --> 00:02:09,422
P G stat statements tells you which one.

56
00:02:09,422 --> 00:02:11,471
Explain analyze tells you why.

57
00:02:12,021 --> 00:02:14,845
An index lets the database jump to the rows.

58
00:02:14,845 --> 00:02:20,171
But always look first: sometimes a query is slow only because it waited for a lock.

59
00:02:21,721 --> 00:02:26,820
Go to the lab folder.

60
00:02:28,370 --> 00:02:38,674
Tell the tools where the server is.

61
00:02:39,224 --> 00:02:41,997
Now P S Q L finds this server by itself.

62
00:02:42,547 --> 00:02:53,598
How many connections are allowed?

63
00:02:54,148 --> 00:02:58,329
Forty. The forty first client is refused. Keep this number in mind.

64
00:02:58,879 --> 00:03:09,460
Who is connected?

65
00:03:12,610 --> 00:03:13,778
Read it by rows.

66
00:03:13,778 --> 00:03:15,966
Cart API: three sessions idle,

67
00:03:15,966 --> 00:03:18,958
that is a web app with a connection pool,

68
00:03:18,958 --> 00:03:20,198
waiting for work.

69
00:03:20,198 --> 00:03:24,211
Cart API active: one session that is running right now.

70
00:03:24,211 --> 00:03:25,232
P S Q L is us.

71
00:03:25,232 --> 00:03:26,326
And report job,

72
00:03:26,326 --> 00:03:27,786
idle in transaction.

73
00:03:27,786 --> 00:03:29,464
That one is suspicious.

74
00:03:30,014 --> 00:03:41,982
Who is waiting for whom?

75
00:03:46,832 --> 00:03:48,976
The last column is the answer.

76
00:03:48,976 --> 00:03:53,695
One session shows a number in braces: it is blocked by that P I D.

77
00:03:53,695 --> 00:03:56,340
And that P I D belongs to report job.

78
00:03:56,340 --> 00:04:00,272
The session that is waiting is the active cart API one.

79
00:04:00,822 --> 00:04:12,278
How long has it waited?

80
00:04:15,928 --> 00:04:16,932
Wait type: lock.

81
00:04:16,932 --> 00:04:19,569
It is waiting for a transaction to finish,

82
00:04:19,569 --> 00:04:21,892
and has been waiting for a long time.

83
00:04:21,892 --> 00:04:24,717
Real customers would be staring at a spinner.

84
00:04:25,267 --> 00:04:33,715
Write down what you found.

85
00:04:37,665 --> 00:04:39,237
Four cart API sessions,

86
00:04:39,237 --> 00:04:40,467
forty connections,

87
00:04:40,467 --> 00:04:41,560
and the blocker.

88
00:04:41,560 --> 00:04:44,840
A command inside dollar and brackets runs first,

89
00:04:44,840 --> 00:04:46,753
and its answer is pasted in.

90
00:04:47,303 --> 00:04:57,095
Try the gentle way.

91
00:04:58,245 --> 00:04:59,973
True. But did anything change?

92
00:05:00,523 --> 00:05:07,734
Is it still there?

93
00:05:10,484 --> 00:05:11,231
Still there,

94
00:05:11,231 --> 00:05:12,850
still idle in transaction.

95
00:05:12,850 --> 00:05:14,967
Cancel only stops a running query,

96
00:05:14,967 --> 00:05:17,208
and this session is running nothing.

97
00:05:17,208 --> 00:05:18,952
It is just holding the lock.

98
00:05:18,952 --> 00:05:20,446
So we need the firm way.

99
00:05:20,996 --> 00:05:31,364
End that one session.

100
00:05:32,214 --> 00:05:36,758
True. The session is gone, its transaction rolled back, and its lock released.

101
00:05:37,308 --> 00:05:40,295
Who is waiting now?

102
00:05:54,145 --> 00:05:55,345
Nobody is blocked.

103
00:05:55,345 --> 00:05:57,480
The waiting update got its lock,

104
00:05:57,480 --> 00:05:57,746
ran,

105
00:05:57,746 --> 00:05:58,613
and finished,

106
00:05:58,613 --> 00:06:01,548
and that cart API session went back to idle.

107
00:06:02,098 --> 00:06:10,076
Did the waiting work finish?

108
00:06:10,626 --> 00:06:15,746
One hundred and one. The waiting update added one. The blocked work was not lost.

109
00:06:16,296 --> 00:06:26,942
Check resource pressure.

110
00:06:33,992 --> 00:06:38,996
A cache hit percentage near ninety nine means reads mostly come from memory.

111
00:06:38,996 --> 00:06:42,091
The database is small: a few tens of megabytes.

112
00:06:42,091 --> 00:06:44,659
On a real server you also watch memory,

113
00:06:44,659 --> 00:06:47,688
disk space and load from the operating system.

114
00:06:48,238 --> 00:06:59,822
Which queries are slow?

115
00:07:03,972 --> 00:07:06,311
The top line is the blocked update.

116
00:07:06,311 --> 00:07:07,581
It looks very slow,

117
00:07:07,581 --> 00:07:10,255
but only because it waited for the lock.

118
00:07:10,255 --> 00:07:11,191
Not its fault.

119
00:07:11,191 --> 00:07:14,667
The second line is the real suspect: a report query,

120
00:07:14,667 --> 00:07:16,137
run twenty five times,

121
00:07:16,137 --> 00:07:18,009
about ten milliseconds each.

122
00:07:18,559 --> 00:07:28,479
Why is it slow?

123
00:07:29,029 --> 00:07:29,631
Seq scan.

124
00:07:29,631 --> 00:07:33,044
The database reads all three hundred thousand rows,

125
00:07:33,044 --> 00:07:35,387
and throws almost all of them away.

126
00:07:35,387 --> 00:07:37,461
Rows removed by filter says it.

127
00:07:37,461 --> 00:07:38,800
That is the problem.

128
00:07:39,350 --> 00:07:50,571
Create an index.

129
00:07:51,121 --> 00:07:54,897
Create index. It took a moment, because it read the table once.

130
00:07:55,447 --> 00:07:58,818
Run the explain again.

131
00:08:04,268 --> 00:08:05,438
Now an index scan.

132
00:08:05,438 --> 00:08:07,975
It jumps straight to the matching rows.

133
00:08:07,975 --> 00:08:11,943
The time dropped from about twenty milliseconds to about one.

134
00:08:11,943 --> 00:08:13,569
Ten times or more faster.

135
00:08:14,119 --> 00:08:16,487
Run the judge.

136
00:08:17,037 --> 00:08:22,669
Five checks. Connections, the blocker, the fix, the index, and the plan. Five out of five.

137
00:08:23,219 --> 00:08:25,182
Clean up.

138
00:08:25,732 --> 00:08:29,444
The server and every session are stopped, and everything is removed.

139
00:08:29,994 --> 00:08:38,506
Is it gone?

140
00:08:39,056 --> 00:08:41,573
No response. Nothing is left behind.

141
00:08:43,123 --> 00:08:43,786
Your task.

142
00:08:43,786 --> 00:08:47,635
A busy shop database has a stuck session and a slow query.

143
00:08:47,635 --> 00:08:48,298
Find them,

144
00:08:48,298 --> 00:08:48,895
fix them,

145
00:08:48,895 --> 00:08:49,758
and prove it.

146
00:08:50,308 --> 00:08:53,048
One: count the sessions named cart API,

147
00:08:53,048 --> 00:08:54,805
and read max connections.

148
00:08:54,805 --> 00:08:57,124
Write both into answers dot text,

149
00:08:57,124 --> 00:08:58,670
line one and line two.

150
00:08:59,220 --> 00:09:03,828
Two: find the session that blocks the others. Write its P I D on line three.

151
00:09:04,378 --> 00:09:08,036
Three: end only that session with P G terminate backend.

152
00:09:08,036 --> 00:09:10,714
The waiting update must finish by itself.

153
00:09:11,264 --> 00:09:14,042
Four: read the plan of the report query,

154
00:09:14,042 --> 00:09:16,541
and create an index on customer I D.

155
00:09:16,541 --> 00:09:17,652
Five: lab check,

156
00:09:17,652 --> 00:09:18,624
then lab down.

157
00:09:20,674 --> 00:09:24,450
